## Why This Is Asked
Tests a classic DP over partitioning with a non-linear segment cost (max). It checks understanding of states, transitions, and reconstruction, plus edge cases like k=1 and k=n.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Segment cost as max over A[j..i-1] and dynamic maintenance of that max
- Reconstruction of endpoints via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minSumMaxPartitions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let mx = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
        mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        if (dp[j][t - 1] !== INF) {
          dp[i][t] = Math.min(dp[i][t], dp[j][t - 1] + mx);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact endpoints efficiently?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) with additional structure or pruning for typical inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests deriving a solvable DP for contiguous partitions with a length-based cost, plus a linear-time optimization and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partition DP
- Prefix sums and O(nk) optimization
- Backtracking to report segment endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTotalWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    let bestVal = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const j = i - 1;
      if (dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] - S[j] + c * j;
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestIdx = j; }
      }
      if (bestVal > -Infinity) {
        dp[i][t] = S[i] - c * i + c + bestVal;
        par[i][t] = bestIdx;
      }
    }
  }
  const segments = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  if (dp[n][k] <= -Infinity/2) return {best: -Infinity, segments: []};
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {best: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if c is paid only for non-first element or per segment boundary?
- How would you extend to maximize total value for at most k segments instead of exactly k?

